{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Why are we to be diminished, so as not to bring…</b> There are those who raise the difficulty: was it hidden from them that one who is impure (טָמֵא) is forbidden to eat consecrated things (קָדָשִׁים)? And this is difficult according to the version in the Talmud (Pesachim 66b), which derives from 'אִישׁ אִישׁ' that an individual is pushed off to the second Pesach but not the community. But according to what is concluded in the Yalkut (section 9b) in the name of the Sifrei — and see Pesachim 77a and Yerushalmi Pesachim 7:4 — that from the superfluity of 'בְּמוֹעֲדוֹ' we learn that the community's Pesach may be offered in impurity (טוּמְאָה), it is not difficult at all. For after they heard the expression 'בְּמוֹעֲדוֹ,' they said: why are we to be diminished from the community? And the answer came: 'אִישׁ אִישׁ' etc. — specifically an individual is pushed off and not the community. And the men understood from the very fact that Moses said to the children of Israel to make the Pesach בְּמוֹעֲדוֹ, even in impurity, and did not say it to them in particularity — hear from this that he excluded them from the general community, for if not so, what emerges from the fact that he said to the children of Israel to make the Pesach? They were already commanded and standing. Rather, he came to exclude the individual impure persons. Therefore they said: 'Why are we to be diminished?'"], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:14.819388384Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}